You are modifying the LEVANTE static dashboard site deployed on Vercel.

## Product intent
- Treat the site as a research operations console, not a marketing page.
- Visual metaphor: mission control / pit wall / research dashboard.
- Prioritize readability, hierarchy, stability, and accessibility.

## Design rules
- Light, low-glare palette: soft cool-gray background with near-white layered surfaces.
- Never use pure white (#FFFFFF) or pure black (#000000).
- Use one main accent color (cyan, dark enough for WCAG AA on light) for active/selected states.
- Use amber or red only for alerts/warnings/exception states.
- Avoid heavy gradients, neon glow, HUD elements, LCARS-style grids.
- Avoid tiny labels and cramped panels.
- Prefer calm spacing, consistent card structures, and minimal chrome.

## Layout rules
- Keep global navigation and header spatially stable across pages.
- Avoid big layout jumps between views.
- Prefer a small number of high-value panels over many noisy widgets.
- Preserve a clear content hierarchy on each page.

## Component rules (static version)
- Introduce design tokens via CSS custom properties in a central stylesheet.
- Use shared classes for cards, panels, headers, badges, tables, and buttons.
- Reuse these utility / component classes across all HTML files.
- Do not introduce a new CSS framework unless explicitly asked.

## Typography
- Minimum body size 16px.
- Use a clear sans-serif stack.
- Maintain heading hierarchy (h1–h3) and concise labels.

## Data viz / infographics
- If charts/images exist, ensure color palettes fit the theme.
- Do not rely on color alone for meaning.
- Prefer legible labels and captions.

## Accessibility
- Maintain visible keyboard focus states on links and controls.
- Meet WCAG AA contrast for text and controls where feasible.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion (disable or tone down animations).
- Ensure clickable/tappable targets are reasonably sized.

## Code change rules
- Start with an audit before refactoring styles.
- Add design tokens in a dedicated theme CSS file.
- Avoid inline styles unless absolutely necessary.
- Keep HTML semantic (headings, landmarks, lists).
- Separate pure visual changes from content or logic changes.

## Output expectations
For each task:
1. List files to inspect.
2. State a short implementation plan.
3. Edit a minimal set of files.
4. Summarize changes and note follow-ups.